# Simple and Compound Interest: Money That Grows on Itself

> Lend a sum at interest and it grows — but *how* it grows changes
> everything. Simple interest pays rent on the original principal
> forever; compound interest pays rent on the rent, and the difference
> over the years becomes spectacular. Einstein is (apocryphally) said
> to have called compounding the eighth wonder of the world; exam
> boards certainly treat it as one.

## 1. Simple Interest

[definition]
**Simple interest (SI)** is computed only on the original principal
$P$ for the entire period, at rate $R\%$ per annum for $T$ years:
[/definition]

@math label="eq:si"
SI = \frac{P \times R \times T}{100}, \qquad A = P\left(1 + \frac{RT}{100}\right)
@/math

Simple interest grows **linearly** — equal increments every year.
$5000$ at $8\%$ for 3 years earns
$\frac{5000 \times 8 \times 3}{100} = 1200$, giving $6200$.

A time-saving view: the interest for one year is
$\frac{PR}{100}$; any fractional period just scales it.

## 2. Compound Interest

[definition]
**Compound interest (CI)** recalculates interest each conversion
period on the *accumulated* amount, not the original principal. With
annual compounding:
[/definition]

@math label="eq:ci"
A = P\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^T, \qquad CI = A - P
@/math

**Worked example.** $5000$ at $8\%$ for 3 years compounded annually:

@math label="eq:ci-example"
A = 5000(1.08)^3 = 5000 \times 1.259712 = 6298.56
@/math

so $CI = 1298.56$ — about $98.56$ more than the simple interest for
the identical terms. That surplus is precisely "interest on
interest."

## 3. Fractional and Multiple Compounding

When interest compounds more than once a year, scale the rate down
and the period count up:

| Compounding | Rate per period | Periods | Amount formula |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| Half-yearly | $\frac{R}{2}$ | $2T$ | $P\left(1+\frac{R}{200}\right)^{2T}$ |
| Quarterly | $\frac{R}{4}$ | $4T$ | $P\left(1+\frac{R}{400}\right)^{4T}$ |
| Monthly | $\frac{R}{12}$ | $12T$ | $P\left(1+\frac{R}{1200}\right)^{12T}$ |

**Worked example.** $10000$ at $10\%$ for 2 years compounded
half-yearly:

@math label="eq:half-yearly"
A = 10000\left(1+\frac{10}{200}\right)^{4} = 10000 \times (1.05)^4 = 12155.06
@/math

More frequent compounding always yields more — watch the limit:
continuous compounding gives $A = Pe^{RT}$.

For fractional years, compound the whole years and apply SI to the
stub: $2\frac{1}{2}$ years at $10\%$ gives
$P(1.1)^2 \times 1.05$.

## 4. The SI-CI Gap

The difference for 2 years is the second year's extra interest —
interest on the first year's interest:

@math label="eq:diff-2yr"
D_2 = P\left(\frac{R}{100}\right)^2
@/math

@math label="eq:diff-3yr"
D_3 = P\left(\frac{R}{100}\right)^2\left(3 + \frac{R}{100}\right)
@/math

**Worked example.** The difference between CI and SI on a sum for
2 years at $10\%$ is $40$. Then
$P\left(\frac{10}{100}\right)^2 = 40$, so
$P = \frac{40 \times 100}{1} \times \frac{100}{100} = 4000$.

[important]
For **one year**, SI and CI are equal (annual compounding) — the gap
only opens from the second year. Questions exploiting this often give
the one-year amounts as identical and ask about year two.
[/important]

## 5. Applications: Population, Depreciation, and Growth

The compound formula models any repeated percentage change:

- **Population growth:** $P_{new} = P\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^n$;
  a $5\%$ annual increase for 2 years on $20000$ gives
  $20000 \times 1.1025 = 22050$.
- **Depreciation:** value falls by $R\%$ yearly,
  $V = P\left(1-\frac{R}{100}\right)^n$; a machine worth $50000$
  depreciating $10\%$ yearly is worth $40500$ after 2 years.
- **Virus/bacteria doubling:** the rate is $100\%$ per period —
  $A = P \cdot 2^n$.

## 6. Instalments and the Rule of 72

**Instalment (EMI) sketch.** A loan $P$ repaid in $n$ equal annual
instalments at rate $R$ satisfies: each instalment $x$ is discounted
back to present value and summed:

@math label="eq:instalment"
P = \frac{x}{1+\frac{R}{100}} + \frac{x}{\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^2} + \cdots + \frac{x}{\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^n}
@/math

**Rule of 72.** Money doubles when
$n \approx \frac{72}{R}$ (percent). At $9\%$, doubling takes about
8 years; at $12\%$, about 6. A sister rule (Rule of 114 for tripling)
extends the trick.

[example]
A sum triples itself at $20\%$ compound interest in approximately
$\log_{1.2} 3 \approx 6.03$ years — but examiners accept the
successive-multiplication route: $1.2^5 \approx 2.49$ and
$1.2^6 \approx 2.99$, so "about 6 years" arrives without logarithms.
[/example]

[summary]
Simple interest is linear: $SI = \frac{PRT}{100}$. Compound interest
is exponential:
$A = P\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^T$, with the rate divided and
periods multiplied for half-yearly ($\frac{R}{200}, 2T$) or quarterly
($\frac{R}{400}, 4T$) compounding. The 2-year SI-CI gap is
$P\left(\frac{R}{100}\right)^2$, the 3-year gap
$P\left(\frac{R}{100}\right)^2\left(3+\frac{R}{100}\right)$; year one
shows no gap. The same engine drives population growth
$\left(1+\frac{R}{100}\right)^n$, depreciation
$\left(1-\frac{R}{100}\right)^n$, doubling problems ($2^n$),
instalment present values, and the Rule of 72 for quick doubling
times. Compounding rewards patience — and punishes borrowers who
ignore it.
[/summary]
